WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.600
Mainstream TV viewers often overlook&nbsp;
horror, but American Horror Story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:13.072
managed to seduce an audience all its own.

00:00:13.072 --> 00:00:16.116
Before American Horror Story debuted in 2011, 

00:00:16.134 --> 00:00:20.920
co-creator Ryan Murphy was best known as&nbsp;
one of the minds behind Glee, a show so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:25.560
happy and positive that it was literally about&nbsp;
a bunch of high school kids gaining self esteem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:30.480
by singing and dancing to familiar pop songs.&nbsp;
Murphy then went directly from his high school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:36.160
musical to a house full of ghosts, violence, and&nbsp;
a Rubber Man. What happened? Who hurt you, Ryan?

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.680
In an interview with Elle, Murphy admitted:&nbsp;
"I was like, 'I can't write any more nice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.200
speeches for these Glee kids about&nbsp;
love and tolerance and togetherness.'"

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:49.400
To that end, he wrote a show about horror and&nbsp;
depravity instead. American Horror Story also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:54.400
allowed Murphy to right a Glee wrong. He'd&nbsp;
wanted Sarah Paulson to play Emma Pillsbury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:59.560
the high school guidance counselor eventually&nbsp;
portrayed by Jayma Mays. Paulson was unable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:04.000
because she was starring in a Broadway play&nbsp;
at the time. Murphy finally found a place for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.560
Paulson as medium Billie Dean Howard, the first&nbsp;
of many memorable AHS roles she would step into.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:19.631
"So much pain here. So much longing and regret."

00:01:19.631 --> 00:01:22.513
While occasionally campy and darkly funny, 

00:01:22.513 --> 00:01:26.840
most every season of American Horror&nbsp;
Story gets pretty bleak pretty quickly.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.960
"I mean, just because you've got 30&nbsp;
bodies buried in your crawl space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:35.000
don't mean you can't have a really terrific&nbsp;
rec room and be a respectable businessman."

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.680
Playing murderers — and their victims — can&nbsp;
really get under the skin of the actors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.640
so a few have tried to relieve&nbsp;
the tension when possible.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:47.040
Zachary Quinto took on a major role in Asylum&nbsp;
as Oliver Thredson. Another role he accepted:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.680
on-set music therapist, playing his banjo&nbsp;
on set to lighten the mood. Lily Rabe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.880
plays guitar in real life, and she'd play to&nbsp;
provide "musical breaks" on tough days too.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:04.720
"Yeah. Musical breaks. It was good. Zach is better&nbsp;
at the banjo than I am at the guitar, for sure."

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.440
Jessica Lange, the grande dame of American&nbsp;
Horror Story, convinced Ryan Murphy to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.320
one of these melodic palette cleansers&nbsp;
into the show itself. After a flashback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.560
revealed that her Asylum character&nbsp;
had once been a singer in a band,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:22.160
Lange suggested a full-on fantasy musical sequence&nbsp;
in the middle of a psychiatric institution — set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:27.360
to the classic '60s novelty song "The Name&nbsp;
Game" — if only because, as she told Vulture:

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.701
"Everything we do is so grim."

00:02:29.701 --> 00:02:32.062
Before it was canceled in 2017, 

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.560
Scream Queens — Ryan Murphy's other&nbsp;
other show — was the place where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:39.840
blood and gore of American Horror Story&nbsp;
met the comedy of Glee. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.760
its first season asked the burning question: How&nbsp;
might a cast made up of self-absorbed sorority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:50.240
girls and dumb frat guys deal with a costumed&nbsp;
serial killer roaming their college campus?

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:56.000
"Good evening, idiot hookers. I'm very happy to&nbsp;
welcome you to hell week here at Kappa House."

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.560
It was also laced with references to classic&nbsp;
slasher movies, tongue-in-cheek horror movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.840
tropes, and it even starred Halloween's&nbsp;
Jamie Lee Curtis in a major role.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:09.200
While that may all sound pretty standard for&nbsp;
the guy who conjured up the Dead Breakfast Club,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:14.000
Scream Queens nearly had even more in common&nbsp;
with American Horror Story. Just how much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.800
you ask? Well, it was almost a spin-off of Coven,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:21.080
the AHS season set at a school&nbsp;
for young witches in New Orleans.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:24.621
"Surprise, bitch. I bet you&nbsp;
thought you'd seen the last of me."

00:03:24.621 --> 00:03:29.360
However, Murphy changed his mind after&nbsp;
a lunch with Fox executives, explaining:

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.520
"We were talking about things that&nbsp;
we loved that were not on the air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.640
things that we loved growing up,&nbsp;
movies that we loved watching."

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:40.240
Once the subject of slasher movies came up,&nbsp;
Scream Queens' own unique identity began to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:45.000
take shape — and while its ride was a short&nbsp;
one, it sure was a blast while it lasted.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.066
"I'm going to kill you now."

00:03:48.066 --> 00:03:51.233
"Wait. Whaaaat???"

00:03:54.246 --> 00:03:56.383
In the months before 

00:03:56.383 --> 00:03:59.640
the sixth season of American&nbsp;
Horror Story premiered in 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:04.320
FX released more than 20 teasers, each&nbsp;
suggesting a totally different theme.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:09.480
At a Television Critics Association&nbsp;
event, FX CEO John Landgraf explained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:14.520
that only one of them accurately represented&nbsp;
the season — while the rest were "misdirects."

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:19.840
Though wildly different, the trailers did share&nbsp;
a few things in common: all of them were creepy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:24.440
weird, and pretty damn unsettling, perhaps&nbsp;
none more so than one that showed a woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:29.120
using some hedge clippers to snip wind&nbsp;
chimes made from human teeth. Turns out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:34.120
that was the real trailer, as that imagery&nbsp;
figures into American Horror Story: Roanoke.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:37.600
In a way, though, all of the fake&nbsp;
trailers were "real." That season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:42.120
of the show explored the notions of "real"&nbsp;
and "fake," as the plot centered in part on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:46.960
a couple who lived in a haunted house near&nbsp;
the lost colony of Roanoke, and a TV show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:52.520
re-creations of the events they endured at&nbsp;
the hands of the ghostly Roanoke colonists.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:57.680
In American Horror Story: Hotel, Evan Peters&nbsp;
portrayed Mr. March, who turned out to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:03.240
arguably the most super evil character in the&nbsp;
show's long list of super evil characters.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:08.461
"I look around, and I see the&nbsp;
definition of American success."

00:05:08.461 --> 00:05:10.880
Why? Well, because he trapped, tortured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:15.040
and killed people in the Hotel Cortez&nbsp;
— duh. It's quite upsetting then that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:20.200
according to Ryan Murphy in Entertainment&nbsp;
Weekly, Mr. March is based on a real person.

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:25.640
That individual is H.H. Holmes, who,&nbsp;
during the Chicago World's Fair in 1893,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:30.560
opened and ran a "hotel of horrors." Visitors&nbsp;
to the great exposition who tragically chose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:35.000
to stay at Holmes' hotel would find&nbsp;
their rooms soundproof and windowless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.600
all the better for Holmes to take&nbsp;
them out via gassing or other means.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:42.800
Secret passageways allowed him to remove&nbsp;
bodies to the basement without detection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:49.640
where he'd destroy the evidence in a furnace. He&nbsp;
was caught in 1894, and executed two years later.

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:53.600
In addition to legendary Oscar winners&nbsp;
like Jessica Lange and Kathy Bates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:58.480
American Horror Story has made stars out of&nbsp;
up-and-comers like Emma Roberts and Evan Peters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:03.360
and proved to the world long before A Star&nbsp;
is Born that Lady Gaga could act. It's hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:07.280
to believe that some performers couldn't or&nbsp;
didn't want to be involved with the top-rated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:12.280
show. Chris Zylka booked a two-episode stint on&nbsp;
Asylum as a deaf man, but a few months later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:15.840
he exited because he didn't want to have to&nbsp;
shave his head, a requirement of the role.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:19.280
Ryan Murphy has also dutifully&nbsp;
pursued a few dream cast members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:25.200
albeit unsuccessfully. Among the targets he has&nbsp;
revealed: Michelle Pfeiffer, Michael Chiklis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.520
and Reese Witherspoon. Talking about the&nbsp;
latter to Entertainment Weekly, Murphy said -

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.920
"I wanted her to play something really twisted&nbsp;
and f***ed up. But she's always booked."

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:39.560
Neil Patrick Harris and his husband, David Burtka,&nbsp;
appeared in Freak Show, although Murphy earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:43.760
pursued the couple for Murder House — he wanted&nbsp;
them to portray the bickering pair ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:48.800
played by Zachary Quinto and Teddy Sears. Harris&nbsp;
explained to Rolling Stone why they skipped out on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:53.200
the part, referring to the couple's roles&nbsp;
in A Very Harold &amp; Kumar 3D Christmas -

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:57.000
"We had just played ourselves as a&nbsp;
couple not getting along. I thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.200
'It just seems weird to do that&nbsp;
twice, as individual actors to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:05.080
play a couple that hate each other twice.&nbsp;
So I said 'no,' that we shouldn't do it."

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:09.640
The eighth season of American Horror Story,&nbsp;
dubbed Apocalypse, tied together characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:14.920
from multiple seasons in the ultimate&nbsp;
crossover event to face the ultimate evil:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:20.920
Michael Langdon, the offspring of Satan – the&nbsp;
antichrist. In the sixth episode of the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:25.400
"Return to Murder House," viewers learn that&nbsp;
Michael realizes his evil abilities in full after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:31.280
a visit from Anton LaVey and his top lieutenants,&nbsp;
who ritually and brutally sacrifice a woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:37.040
remove her beating heart, and give it over to&nbsp;
the young embodiment of true evil to feast upon.

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:42.600
It was a deeply upsetting scene for many viewers,&nbsp;
but in a different way for the real-life Church&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:47.720
of Satan. Anton LaVey was a real person, who&nbsp;
really did found the Church of Satan and wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:52.880
several books espousing the beliefs of the&nbsp;
10,000-strong organization. Present-day COS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:57.240
leaders objected to the portrayal of the church&nbsp;
on American Horror Story, tweeting about their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:03.160
disapproval in October 2018 and pointing&nbsp;
out the inaccuracies in the adaptation.

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:06.440
When Ryan Murphy talked to The Hollywood&nbsp;
Reporter regarding Sarah Paulson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:10.600
portrayal of Bette and Dot Tattler during&nbsp;
the fourth season of American Horror Story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:13.960
he couldn't hold back his&nbsp;
overwhelming praise, saying -

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:20.000
"It's absolutely the most challenging&nbsp;
thing I've ever seen any actor do ever."

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:23.120
"It was really terrifying as you might&nbsp;
imagine - I didn't know how in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:29.000
world we were going to do it and it's a&nbsp;
really scary thing to act by yourself."

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:34.200
The dual role found Paulson playing conjoined&nbsp;
twins with two heads and one body, living in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:39.200
traveling sideshow on American Horror Story: Freak&nbsp;
Show. It was also one of the most challenging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:44.720
things the show's special effects artists ever&nbsp;
had to attempt. It required a lot of old-fashioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:50.440
trickery, labor, and digital manipulation for&nbsp;
Paulson to convincingly play both characters.

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:53.480
First, the effects team took&nbsp;
a mold of Paulson's own head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:57.400
and used it to build two sophisticated&nbsp;
prosthetic heads, as Murphy explained -

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:02.000
"So when Sarah is doing her coverage where&nbsp;
she's Dot, she has on a fake head to her left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:06.920
which is Bette, and it moves. She&nbsp;
literally has to do scenes with herself."

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:12.480
Those scenes took as long as 15 hours to shoot,&nbsp;
because everything with the Tattlers had to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:19.520
filmed for close-up, in medium shots, for&nbsp;
effects, and from various angles...twice.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:25.280
American Horror Story: Freak Show takes place in&nbsp;
Florida among the denizens of one of few remaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:30.520
"Cabinet of Curiosities" shows in 1952. A&nbsp;
highlight of the season is the recurring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:36.000
motif of anachronistic music from musicians like&nbsp;
Nirvana and David Bowie, meaning characters set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.280
in the show's world of the early '50s sing&nbsp;
very famous songs recorded decades later.

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:45.280
Ryan Murphy explained that the songs&nbsp;
were carefully selected for Freak Show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:47.800
and for good reason, as he explained -

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:51.000
"We decided we only were going&nbsp;
to highlight musical artists who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:54.600
at some point in their career had&nbsp;
identified themselves as feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:59.554
like freaks or misfits or outcasts,&nbsp;
which our people are going through."

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:04.320
American Horror Story: Hotel took inspiration&nbsp;
in part from the ghastly horrors committed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:10.120
H.H. Holmes, and seemingly from something more&nbsp;
modern and ambiguous as well. The Hotel Cortez&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:15.600
is loosely based on the Cecil Hotel, a structure&nbsp;
in the "skid row" area of downtown Los Angeles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:21.760
that's hosted countless tourists and transients&nbsp;
as well as murderers and mysteries. Austrian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:26.640
serial killer Jack Unterweger resided at the&nbsp;
hotel for a while, as did Richard Ramirez,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:31.720
the murderer known as the Night Stalker. He's&nbsp;
even a character in American Horror Story: Hotel.

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:36.480
The one incident that really moved the needle&nbsp;
for American Horror Story creator Ryan Murphy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:42.120
was the story of Elisa Lam. As explored in&nbsp;
the 2021 Netflix documentary Crime Scene:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:46.920
The Vanishing at the Cecil Hotel, Canadian&nbsp;
student Lam stayed at the Cecil during a trip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:53.080
to Los Angeles in 2013... only to disappear.&nbsp;
An internet-driven manhunt ensued, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:57.680
after the release of a bizarre surveillance&nbsp;
video of Lam acting erratically in a Cecil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:03.360
Hotel elevator. A few days later, Lam's body was&nbsp;
discovered in a rooftop water tank. Murphy summed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:08.680
up the puzzling circumstances at a Television&nbsp;
Critics Association event in August 2015 -

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:13.960
"A girl got in an elevator in a downtown&nbsp;
hotel. She was never seen again."

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:19.800
The sinister-sounding, postmodern opening sequence&nbsp;
for American Horror Story seems tailor-made for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:26.360
the show, a combination of spooky, forbidding,&nbsp;
and hypnotic musical themes punctuated by jarring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.360 --> 00:11:31.840
jump-scare sounds. Surprisingly, the bulk of&nbsp;
it comes from a 20-year-old class project.

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:36.120
Back in 1998, César Dávila-Irizarry&nbsp;
was a sophomore at the University of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:40.400
Puerto Rico. For a music history class, he&nbsp;
created a primitive digital audio track,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:45.680
using Cool Edit 96 on his Windows&nbsp;
98 computer, stretching, mixing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:51.080
and combining various sounds with white noise.&nbsp;
For example, the song's first dissonant moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:55.200
comes from a clip of Dávila-Irizarry&nbsp;
dropping wire hangers on a tile floor.

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:59.204
When he was done, Dávila-Irizarry&nbsp;
gave it to his friend, Gabriel Diaz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.204 --> 00:12:02.280
who went on to work as a video editor&nbsp;
on the first season of American Horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:07.200
Story. He used Dávila-Irizarry's track as a&nbsp;
placeholder, but producers liked it so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:12.000
they decided to keep it. For technical and&nbsp;
legal reasons, show composer Charlie Clouser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:17.000
of Nine Inch Nails recreated and spruced&nbsp;
it up with Dávila-Irizarry's blessing.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:22.040
Apart from a couple of appearances as Church of&nbsp;
Satan cardinal Samantha Crowe in the Apocalypse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:26.440
season, actor Naomi Grossman left her mark&nbsp;
on the American Horror Story franchise in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:31.880
the second-season Asylum story. She portrayed&nbsp;
Pepper, a woman who suffers from microcephaly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:36.440
which means someone having a smaller than normal&nbsp;
head. She also possesses many other notable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:41.880
physical traits, such as large ears, jutting&nbsp;
teeth, a mostly shaved pate, and a top-knot.

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:46.160
Grossman is almost unrecognizable as&nbsp;
Pepper, and her physical transformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:50.760
into the character for each episode of&nbsp;
Asylum was a long and involved process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:53.040
with Grossman telling Entertainment Weekly -

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:56.160
"The entire process takes about&nbsp;
two and a half to three hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:58.840
and that's with two men working the entire time!"

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:01.400
Freckles and veins were drawn on Grossman's skin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:04.400
and then came extensive&nbsp;
prosthetics, which included -

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:07.360
"a forehead complete with punched-in eyebrows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:11.960
a nose, ears, and even a bumpy&nbsp;
little spine-piece for my back."

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:14.440
Grossman was also outfitted&nbsp;
for a set of false teeth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:18.000
which altered the shape of her mouth&nbsp;
and face. Grossman additionally revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:22.840
that she couldn't even use one of her&nbsp;
eyes thanks to a lens she had to wear.

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:26.880
Songs recorded by Fleetwood Mac, as&nbsp;
well as that band's Stevie Nicks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:31.400
permeate the soundtracks of multiple American&nbsp;
Horror Story seasons. That's logical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:34.080
as Nicks has long been associated with witches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:39.480
dark spirits, and other spooky stuff that's&nbsp;
abundant in the Ryan Murphy horror anthology.

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:44.480
Nicks' bewitching presence is felt most&nbsp;
strongly in the third American Horror Story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:50.480
subtitled Coven, where Lily Rabe plays a witch who&nbsp;
deeply loves Nicks' music even before she finds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:56.200
out the singer is an actual, practicing witch,&nbsp;
too. Nicks makes a cameo late in the season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.200 --> 00:13:59.680
and it was tough for American Horror&nbsp;
Story producers to make it happen.

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:04.240
Nicks was friendly with AHS creators Ryan&nbsp;
Murphy and Brad Falchuck, having lent a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:09.040
hand on a Fleetwood Mac tribute episode of&nbsp;
their series Glee. For American Horror Story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:14.040
Nicks told the Los Angeles Times that the creators&nbsp;
asked if they could use her music again, and when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:18.680
they explained that her songs were an integral&nbsp;
aspect for the lonely character Misty Day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:24.800
Nicks acquiesced. When Murphy asked her to appear,&nbsp;
she was terrified at first — she had a terrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:31.720
time acting in a fourth grade play and had,&nbsp;
quote, "vowed to stay away" from acting forever.

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:36.106
"This shawl has danced across&nbsp;
the stages of the world and now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.106 --> 00:14:40.906
it's yours. And good luck with the seven wonders."

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:43.960
Each year, the story, setting,&nbsp;
and characters are different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:48.960
but the actors remain the same. From the beginning&nbsp;
of the American Horror Story anthology concept,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:53.520
creator Ryan Murphy has utilized a collective&nbsp;
of performers, with many of them dutifully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:59.640
returning for each successive season. However,&nbsp;
when it came time to shoot season nine, 1984,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:04.120
Murphy had to make do without the use&nbsp;
of some of his most venerable actors.

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:08.000
For example, Evan Peters starred in&nbsp;
eight seasons, then left the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:12.720
citing burnout and an aversion to getting&nbsp;
typecast in horror roles. Sarah Paulson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:17.840
also appeared in each of the first eight tales,&nbsp;
and Jessica Lange bowed out after four seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:23.000
then had a small, role-reprising cameo in the&nbsp;
eighth season, which tied several different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:27.800
disparate American Horror stories together.&nbsp;
It also involved a large number of actors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:32.560
which stretched FX's budget, with FX&nbsp;
CEO Jon Landgraf telling TheWrap -

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:37.280
"It was a monster in terms of the&nbsp;
size of the cast, cost of that cast,"

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:39.520
According to Landgraf, Murphy had to quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:44.760
"clean the slate, start over." When&nbsp;
American Horror Story: 1984 debuted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:50.760
it boasted a cast composed mostly of new&nbsp;
and latter-day arrivals to the franchise.

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:54.520
After the soft reboot of American Horror&nbsp;
Story was announced, in the form of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.520 --> 00:16:00.320
'80s horror movie-influenced season called&nbsp;
1984, a series fan and video editor named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:04.800
Corey Vega made a preemptive cut of what he&nbsp;
thought the upcoming episodes' opening credit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:09.600
sequence ought to look like. In short, Vega&nbsp;
thought it should look extremely '80s. He put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:13.880
together a rapid-fire clip montage that&nbsp;
included shots of aerobics instructors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:18.520
neon-colored cars, moonwalking, and neon&nbsp;
graphics. And because it's supposed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:23.080
an American Horror Story clip, lots&nbsp;
of blood and gore permeated it all.

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:27.400
The video spread around the internet until&nbsp;
it reached a very influential figure:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:32.920
co-creator and producer Ryan Murphy. Murphy wrote&nbsp;
on Instagram that he was such a fan of the edit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:39.880
that he hired Vega to recreate his video with&nbsp;
AHS titles designer Kyle Cooper to use in 1984.

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:43.880
Lots of people have a fear of clowns, known&nbsp;
as coulrophobia. It must be something about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:49.680
their frozen, exaggerated, and untrustworthy&nbsp;
grins, or how their identity is concealed under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:54.480
white face paint. American Horror Story aptly&nbsp;
exploited those fears in its Freak Show season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:59.560
featuring John Carroll Lynch as the monstrous&nbsp;
Twisty the Clown, who isn't so much a children's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:05.040
party entertainer as he is a guy who kills people&nbsp;
with scissors and locks kids in an old school bus.

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:09.520
Perhaps unsurprisingly, this subplot&nbsp;
upset some real-life clowns, as Clowns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:14.080
of America president Glenn Kohlberger&nbsp;
said in response to Freak Show in 2014 -

00:17:14.080 --> 00:17:18.440
"Hollywood makes money sensationalizing&nbsp;
the norm. They can take any situation no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:22.560
matter how good or pure and turn it&nbsp;
into a nightmare. We do not support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:28.480
in any way, shape, or form any medium that&nbsp;
sensationalizes or adds to coulrophobia."

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:33.000
The protests flared up again in 2017, when&nbsp;
American Horror Story: Cult, once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:38.480
featured evil clowns. World Clown Association&nbsp;
president Pam Moody told the Detroit Free Press -

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:42.240
"The role of a clown persona,&nbsp;
regardless of their style of clowning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:46.722
is meant to bring joy and laughter,&nbsp;
to bring humor to a hurting world."

